1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,306
A plant on a window sill
bends toward the light.

2
00:00:03,306 --> 00:00:05,107
A trimmed hedge grows bushier.

3
00:00:05,507 --> 00:00:09,201
And some rice seedlings grow so
tall and thin that they fall over.

4
00:00:09,201 --> 00:00:11,786
All three are run by
tiny chemical messengers.

5
00:00:12,236 --> 00:00:14,312
Aarav turned his window plant around.

6
00:00:14,312 --> 00:00:16,885
In a few days it bent
back toward the light.

7
00:00:16,885 --> 00:00:18,707
He asked: what makes it bend?

8
00:00:18,707 --> 00:00:20,572
And which part senses the light?

9
00:00:20,922 --> 00:00:22,338
Here is the short answer.

10
00:00:22,338 --> 00:00:24,382
Chemicals called plant growth regulators.

11
00:00:24,382 --> 00:00:27,465
And the shoot tip senses the
light and sends a signal down.

12
00:00:27,965 --> 00:00:31,248
By the end, you will know what plant
growth regulators are, how auxin was

13
00:00:31,248 --> 00:00:33,270
found, and what auxins
and gibberellins do.

14
00:00:33,920 --> 00:00:35,562
First, what are plant growth regulators?

15
00:00:35,912 --> 00:00:38,983
They are small, simple molecules,
also called plant hormones.

16
00:00:38,983 --> 00:00:40,503
Their chemistry varies a lot.

17
00:00:40,953 --> 00:00:43,085
Some are indole compounds, like IAA.

18
00:00:43,085 --> 00:00:45,259
Some come from adenine, like kinetin.

19
00:00:45,259 --> 00:00:47,909
Some come from carotenoids,
like abscisic acid.

20
00:00:47,909 --> 00:00:49,914
Some are terpenes, like GA three.

21
00:00:49,914 --> 00:00:51,664
And one is a gas, ethylene.

22
00:00:52,114 --> 00:00:54,450
Auxins, gibberellins and
cytokinins mostly promote growth.

23
00:00:54,450 --> 00:00:55,717
Abscisic acid inhibits growth.

24
00:00:55,717 --> 00:00:57,545
Ethylene can do both, but mostly inhibits.

25
00:00:58,195 --> 00:01:00,394
Let's zoom into a young grass seedling.

26
00:01:00,744 --> 00:01:04,895
A grass seedling first pushes up a
sheath that covers its young shoot.

27
00:01:04,895 --> 00:01:06,774
This sheath is the coleoptile.

28
00:01:07,274 --> 00:01:08,182
Zoom closer.

29
00:01:08,182 --> 00:01:11,599
Light from one side makes the
coleoptile bend toward it.

30
00:01:11,599 --> 00:01:14,230
Growing toward light
is called phototropism.

31
00:01:14,730 --> 00:01:16,035
Zoom to the tip.

32
00:01:16,035 --> 00:01:18,485
The tip is where the signal starts.

33
00:01:18,485 --> 00:01:21,602
It travels down and the
whole coleoptile bends.

34
00:01:22,252 --> 00:01:23,392
How was this found?

35
00:01:23,392 --> 00:01:25,300
Follow the experiments step by step.

36
00:01:25,650 --> 00:01:26,550
Step one.

37
00:01:26,285 --> 00:01:29,542
Charles Darwin and his son Francis
Darwin lit canary grass coleoptiles

38
00:01:29,542 --> 00:01:30,442
from one side.

39
00:01:30,416 --> 00:01:31,886
They bent toward the light.

40
00:01:32,636 --> 00:01:33,536
Step two.

41
00:01:33,390 --> 00:01:34,806
They covered the tip.

42
00:01:34,806 --> 00:01:36,838
Now the coleoptile did not bend.

43
00:01:37,588 --> 00:01:38,488
Step three.

44
00:01:38,441 --> 00:01:39,924
They cut off the tip.

45
00:01:39,924 --> 00:01:41,249
Again, no bending.

46
00:01:41,249 --> 00:01:44,781
So the tip sends a signal that
bends the whole coleoptile.

47
00:01:45,531 --> 00:01:46,431
Step four.

48
00:01:46,283 --> 00:01:47,183
Later, F.

49
00:01:47,079 --> 00:01:47,979
W.

50
00:01:47,433 --> 00:01:51,289
Went isolated this signal, auxin,
from the tips of oat coleoptiles.

51
00:01:52,039 --> 00:01:52,939
Step five.

52
00:01:52,785 --> 00:01:56,095
The word auxin comes from the
Greek word auxein, to grow.

53
00:01:56,095 --> 00:01:58,341
It was first isolated from human urine.

54
00:01:59,091 --> 00:02:00,178
Now, meet the auxins.

55
00:02:00,528 --> 00:02:03,341
IAA and IBA are natural
auxins, found in plants.

56
00:02:03,341 --> 00:02:06,247
NAA and two four D are
synthetic, made by people.

57
00:02:06,697 --> 00:02:10,646
Auxins are made at the growing tips
of stems and roots, and move to where

58
00:02:10,646 --> 00:02:11,546
they act.

59
00:02:11,785 --> 00:02:13,716
They help stem cuttings grow roots.

60
00:02:13,716 --> 00:02:16,393
They promote flowering,
for example in pineapples.

61
00:02:16,843 --> 00:02:21,079
They stop young leaves and fruits from
dropping early, but make old leaves

62
00:02:21,079 --> 00:02:22,204
and fruits fall.

63
00:02:22,654 --> 00:02:25,213
The shoot tip holds back the side buds.

64
00:02:25,213 --> 00:02:26,875
This is apical dominance.

65
00:02:26,875 --> 00:02:29,634
Cut off the tip, and the side buds grow.

66
00:02:29,634 --> 00:02:32,584
That is why tea bushes
and hedges are trimmed.

67
00:02:33,034 --> 00:02:37,649
Auxins can make fruits form without
fertilisation, called parthenocarpy,

68
00:02:37,649 --> 00:02:38,890
as in tomatoes.

69
00:02:38,890 --> 00:02:43,704
And two four D kills broad-leaved dicot
weeds but spares grasses, so lawns

70
00:02:43,704 --> 00:02:44,829
stay weed-free.

71
00:02:45,479 --> 00:02:46,562
Now, the gibberellins.

72
00:02:46,562 --> 00:02:48,302
Their story starts with a rice disease.

73
00:02:48,652 --> 00:02:52,669
In the bakane, or foolish seedling,
disease, rice seedlings grow too tall.

74
00:02:52,669 --> 00:02:55,240
It is caused by a fungus,
Gibberella fujikuroi.

75
00:02:55,690 --> 00:02:56,590
E.

76
00:02:56,063 --> 00:02:59,939
Kurosawa treated healthy rice seedlings
with a germ-free liquid from the

77
00:02:59,939 --> 00:03:00,839
fungus.

78
00:03:00,540 --> 00:03:02,405
They showed the same symptoms.

79
00:03:02,405 --> 00:03:04,871
The active substance was gibberellic acid.

80
00:03:05,321 --> 00:03:09,714
More than one hundred gibberellins
are known, named GA one, GA two, GA

81
00:03:09,714 --> 00:03:10,938
three and so on.

82
00:03:10,938 --> 00:03:12,893
GA three is the most studied.

83
00:03:12,893 --> 00:03:14,506
All of them are acidic.

84
00:03:14,956 --> 00:03:16,880
Gibberellins make the stem longer.

85
00:03:16,880 --> 00:03:20,694
They lengthen grape stalks, and make
apples longer and better shaped.

86
00:03:20,694 --> 00:03:23,772
They delay ageing, so fruits
stay on the tree longer.

87
00:03:24,222 --> 00:03:26,605
GA three speeds up malting in brewing.

88
00:03:26,605 --> 00:03:30,648
Sprayed on sugarcane, gibberellins
lengthen the stem and raise yield by

89
00:03:30,648 --> 00:03:32,846
as much as twenty tonnes per acre.

90
00:03:33,296 --> 00:03:35,733
They help young conifers make seeds early.

91
00:03:35,733 --> 00:03:39,895
And they cause bolting, a quick stretch
of the stem just before flowering,

92
00:03:39,895 --> 00:03:41,206
in beet and cabbage.

93
00:03:41,656 --> 00:03:42,556
Your turn.

94
00:03:42,449 --> 00:03:44,754
A gardener cuts the top off a hedge.

95
00:03:44,754 --> 00:03:46,393
Why does it grow bushier?

96
00:03:46,393 --> 00:03:47,559
Pause and decide.

97
00:03:50,759 --> 00:03:53,149
The tip was holding back the side buds.

98
00:03:53,149 --> 00:03:55,584
Remove it, and the side buds grow out.

99
00:03:56,234 --> 00:03:57,199
Remember this.

100
00:03:57,199 --> 00:03:59,616
The shoot tip holds back the side buds.

101
00:04:00,216 --> 00:04:01,806
Now, a worked example.

102
00:04:01,806 --> 00:04:04,702
Pick the right regulator
for each farm problem.

103
00:04:05,052 --> 00:04:06,008
Problem one.

104
00:04:06,008 --> 00:04:08,237
A lawn full of broad-leaved weeds.

105
00:04:08,237 --> 00:04:10,170
Use two four D, an auxin.

106
00:04:10,620 --> 00:04:11,520
Problem two.

107
00:04:11,421 --> 00:04:12,941
Sugarcane with short stems.

108
00:04:12,941 --> 00:04:14,883
Spray gibberellins to lengthen them.

109
00:04:15,333 --> 00:04:16,403
Problem three.

110
00:04:16,403 --> 00:04:18,045
Tomatoes with no seeds.

111
00:04:18,045 --> 00:04:21,427
Use an auxin, so fruit
forms without fertilisation.

112
00:04:22,077 --> 00:04:23,541
Now, the exam trap.

113
00:04:23,891 --> 00:04:27,212
Many students think two four D
kills every plant it touches.

114
00:04:27,612 --> 00:04:29,870
But two four D removes dicot weeds.

115
00:04:29,870 --> 00:04:33,116
Mature monocots, like lawn
grass, are not affected.

116
00:04:33,766 --> 00:04:35,214
Where is this asked?

117
00:04:35,214 --> 00:04:39,462
As match and odd-one-out questions on
auxin and gibberellin uses and their

118
00:04:39,462 --> 00:04:40,362
discovery.

119
00:04:40,850 --> 00:04:41,997
Exam level now.

120
00:04:41,997 --> 00:04:44,340
Which pair is not correctly matched?

121
00:04:44,340 --> 00:04:45,537
Pause and solve.

122
00:04:50,937 --> 00:04:52,336
The answer is C.

123
00:04:52,336 --> 00:04:54,570
GA three does not kill weeds.

124
00:04:54,570 --> 00:04:55,969
Two four D does.

125
00:04:56,469 --> 00:04:59,971
IAA is a natural auxin,
and NAA is a synthetic one.

126
00:05:00,471 --> 00:05:04,363
Gibberellins cause bolting in beet
and cabbage, so pair D is correct.

127
00:05:04,963 --> 00:05:06,503
Here is your recap.

128
00:05:06,503 --> 00:05:08,086
Screenshot this card.

129
00:05:11,286 --> 00:05:13,472
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

130
00:05:13,872 --> 00:05:14,772
One.

131
00:05:14,342 --> 00:05:15,948
What is apical dominance?

132
00:05:16,848 --> 00:05:17,748
Two.

133
00:05:17,368 --> 00:05:19,880
Who isolated auxin, and from where?

134
00:05:20,780 --> 00:05:21,680
Three.

135
00:05:21,289 --> 00:05:22,317
What is bolting?

136
00:05:23,417 --> 00:05:27,426
Next time: why does a raw banana
ripen faster beside a ripe one?

137
00:05:27,426 --> 00:05:30,750
We find out in cytokinins,
ethylene and abscisic acid.
